# /plan

**Project overlay:** if `docs/process/commands/plan.local.md` exists, read it
before you start — the project's own rules for this command, binding over
this file where it says so; a template doc this command sends you to may
have one beside it (`<doc>.local.md`), read with the doc
(`docs/process/overlays.md`).

Turn an approved specification into a testable task breakdown that an
agent can execute without more context.
{% if modules.speckit %}Run `/speckit-plan`, then `/speckit-tasks`; add the `tier: N` and
`issue: <ref>` lines to `specs/<feature>/plan.md` (the review and issue
gates key on them — `docs/process/journal-state-plans.md`). The input spec
must be clarification-free (the clarification gate blocks unresolved
markers here). Run `/speckit-analyze` at plan exit for Tier 3 or a heavily
iterated spec (`docs/process/modules/speckit.md`, token economy).

**Task grain: one task = one behaviour, test AND implementation.** Do not
split "write the failing test" and "implement" into two tasks — TDD order
lives *inside* the task (failing test first, watched, then the code; two
commits stay fine). The task is the unit of planning, checkboxes, gate
runs, and reporting, and every task pays that ritual overhead once — a
50-task list that could be 30 pays it twenty times for nothing. Split
tasks by ownership and dependency, never by TDD phase. An expected review
surface above the bundle size limit (`/review`, "Size") is split along
contracts into stacked plans (*phase-of*).

**A `[P]` marker is a no-shared-touchpoint claim, not a vibe.** Disjoint
files are necessary, not sufficient. Mark two tasks parallel only when
their file sets are fully disjoint — including barrel exports, manifests,
lockfiles, generated indexes and files, and shared CSS: two tasks that each
add one line to the same file are sequential — AND they share no other
resource: database state, ports, fixtures. A producer and its consumer may
run in parallel only once their shared contract (schema, error behaviour,
timing) is fixed in the plan; otherwise clarify it first or serialise them.
The execute engine dispatches `[P]` groups as concurrent subagents on the
marker's word (`/execute`, "parallel edit, serial commit"), so a wrong
marker becomes a collision, not a style issue. Every task names its exact
files; derive the markers from those sets. Existing isolation, test lanes
and serial commits are the means — and the integrated combination is still
tested: green packages do not replace it. Example: an export endpoint and
the importer reading its files touch disjoint files, but the file format is
still open — no `[P]`. Once the format and its error cases are fixed and
each task uses its own fixtures and no shared port — `[P]`.
Mark a task `[mechanical]` or `[design]` when its class is clear — the
model its spawn names follows it (`docs/process/tower.md`, "Model by task class").
{% else %}Read `docs/process/workflow.md` (Plan)
and `docs/process/journal-state-plans.md` for where plans live. The input design
must be clarification-free — no unresolved `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION]` marker may
survive into the plan (the `clarification` gate blocks it). Write small
tasks with concrete files, code, and test commands. Required for every Tier 2+
change. One task = one behaviour, test and implementation (failing test first,
inside the task); a parallel marker needs disjoint files and a fixed shared
contract with no shared resource (`docs/process/workflow.md`, Plan).
{% endif %}
**A rename, removal or move is inventoried here, before any code — and so is
every change to something others read:** a version, path or constant (an
image tag, a pinned version, a port, a path that tests, docs or a Makefile
spell out), a file format or ledger line, a default or timeout (also across
a process or client boundary), a file layout, an environment variable, an
assumption about which process something runs in. For each, find what
depends on it — readers and writers, callers, rules, IDs, unit/end-to-end/visual tests with
their baselines, docs, registries — and put each into the existing task
that changes it (its file list); the tasks stay the one inventory. The
search itself goes into `## Consumers`: per changed item the command and its
hits (`path:line`), nothing else — a grep for the identifier alone misses
most of them, and a blast radius asserted without one is not evidence
(`docs/process/review-checklist.md`, Completeness). A moved rule's task also names where
each of its duties is fulfilled afterwards (`duty: <what> → <new owner>,
pinned by <test>`). Example — moving the "marker in a plan blocks" check
from gate A to gate B: the task lists gate B's check and its test, gate A's
removed check and its test, the doc that names gate A as the owner, and
the gate registry entry, plus `duty: marker in plan blocks → gate B, pinned
by test_marker_in_plan_is_hard`. The same holds for a change to how a
fact is computed (an issue number, a target, a tier): grep every reader of
that fact, name its one owner, and give each reader's task the move to ask
that owner — a second reader left behind judges the next input differently
(`docs/process/failure-catalog.md`). `/execute` compares the final diff against
this inventory before `pushed`; it does not build a second one.

**Known facts come with the task.** Where the project keeps generated
inventories (`docs/process/inventories.md`), run `uv run
scripts/process/inventory.py for-plan <plan>` once the files are named: every
listed row a task touches — an environment variable, a job and the process it
runs in, a table, a tool — goes into that task with its `path:line`. The fact
was already written down; the plan makes sure it is read.

**A change a user notices brings its news line.** A task writes the fragment
(`uv run scripts/process/news.py new --type new|improved|fixed --slug <slug>
"<one to three plain sentences>"`, `docs/process/news.md`) — what changes for
the user, no file names or jargon. Internal work (refactoring, tests,
process) has none.

**A migration shows its fresh worktree first.** A plan that migrates
(`template_verify` reports `migration: true`) or deletes or replaces a gate,
hook or lane script carries `## Fresh-worktree dry run`
(`docs/process/design-template.md`) before its first plan review: its steps
or their checks run once in a fresh `git worktree` without `.venv` or
`node_modules`, with commands and exit codes, and a consumer search of every
removed path, existence probes included (`docs/process/failure-catalog.md`,
"Fresh worktree"). Assumptions about a warm tree are what the plan review
otherwise finds, one round at a time.

**The tier carries its reason.** Name the boundary the change touches and
the guard that enforces it independently of this diff (`file:line`); when the
changed code is the only place a boundary is enforced, the change is Tier 3
(`docs/process/review-checklist.md`, "Does the declared tier hold?").

**The plan decides what the review will check.** Each section of
`docs/process/review-checklist.md` opens with a **Plan decides:** line;
for every dimension your change touches, the plan names that decision —
bounds and a deadline for the whole operation, the error and return shape
of each new interface, where untrusted input is validated, the countermeasure
and test for each spec risk, the test per acceptance criterion. Every
signature, schema or API the plan cites is checked against the code
(`file:line`), not recalled. No "n/a" rows: name only what the change touches.

**Plan coherent work packets for the smallest suitable model.** Every
decision the plan takes is one an executor does not have to. Classify a task
`[mechanical]` when behaviour and interfaces are decided — exact files, the
signature or contract to implement, the test with what makes it red, the
command that proves it — and it runs on the policy's mechanical cell, a small
model (`docs/process/tower.md`, "Model by task class"). A `[design]` task names
its remaining judgment in one line; separate that judgment from the
mechanical rest where this reduces work. Choose coherent packets, not a fresh
agent per tiny step: a packet split finer than its context costs more briefing
than it saves.

**Each packet says who executes it.** For each substantial packet record
`Execution: delegate | retain-context` and one sentence on context transfer
and independent verifiability. Delegate when settled decisions, a compact
source packet and a checkable result make the saved work exceed briefing,
rereading, questions, integration and checking. Retain context for tightly
coupled diagnosis or decisions, expensive context reconstruction, frequent
expected questions, or a small remaining tail. Reconsider only when the
packet's scope or context needs change materially. The task class decides a
handoff's model; it neither forces a handoff nor changes the running model.

**Every task carries its anchors, so execute does not search.** A small
execute model spends most of its turns finding code and re-running tests
(measured downstream: 52 % of tool calls were `grep`/`sed`/`cat` slices,
30 % test runs, 9 % edits; with anchored tasks the median run took 53 turns
instead of 183, and the search share fell to 25 %, #221). The plan pays that
search once, on the planning model. Each task names: the file, function and
`file:line` range it changes, with the current excerpt quoted when the edit is
non-trivial; the one narrow command that proves it (`pytest path::name`,
`vitest run path`) and what is red before and green after; for a new
interface or a tricky edit, the signature or a test skeleton. Anchors are
measured on the plan's base (`file:line`), not recalled.

**A plan is reviewed before `planned` (Tier 2+).** Commit the plan, then
build its bundle (`uv run scripts/process/make_review_bundle.py --plan <slug> --plan-review
-o .process-work/reviews/<slug>-plan.bundle.md`). **Tier 2:** hand the bundle
to a fresh subagent that did not write the plan, with the plan brief of
`docs/process/refute.md` ("Refuting a plan") — owner, cited claims, fail-open,
edge cases, the checklist's Plan halves — on a model of no lower class than
this session's (`review_evidence.ranks` in `model-policy.json`: a smaller
reviewer misses what the planner could not see either). The plan carries the
line `plan-session model: <your concrete model id>`, written when you write
the plan. A plan that names gate code (`scripts/process/`, hooks, CI, the
Makefile) is reviewed on a second model family where one is available, with
the same brief in the same run. Blocks
follow `verification-independence.md` ("What blocks"): a failure scenario,
scored 80 or more. Fix and commit the plan, rebuild the bundle and let the
subagent check the fixes; then attest the pass on that bundle and commit it:
`attest.py --plan-review --work <id> --tier 2 --model <reviewer's concrete model id>
--independence bundle,non-implementing[,cross-model] --verdict pass --bundle <bundle>
--commit`. `attest.py` refuses a reviewer of a lower class, an unknown model
id, and a gate-code plan reviewed by one family without the line
`skipped cross-model work=<id>-plan: <reason>` (and `single-family` in the
independence); `skipped plan-review work=<id>-plan: <reason>` is the
countable way past the class rule. A skip line belongs in the plan — committed
before the bundle, or appended after the plan's last line and attested with
`--with` the plan; anywhere else it counts for nothing. A pass counts for the plan text it reviewed — edit the plan after
it, and it is due again. **Tier 3:** report `planned` without it — dispatch
queues a plan-review session on a second model family, and execute only
after its pass. Where the review phase runs on another host, push the branch
first; the steward then queues execute after the pass. Dispatch queues no
execute for a Tier 2+ plan without a clearing plan pass.

When the plan is committed, run the gates (`uv run scripts/process/gate_runner.py`) and fix in the plan what it turns red — a plan declaration a gate misses turns every execute push red, and dispatch queues no execute while they are red. Then `uv run scripts/process/report.py planned --issue N` (`/report`) — one line to the tower. An open question for the owner goes into the plan as `DECISION NEEDED <date> <you>: <question> — options: …; recommendation: …` plus `report.py blocked`; the steward relays it and writes the answer back as a `DECISION` line. Whoever answers marks the question too — `(answered <date>)` before its colon, or the `DECISION` line in its place: an answer below an unmarked question leaves it open, and the train refuses the branch.

**Open the `## Decisions` ledger in the plan** (`journal-state-plans.md`,
Plans): one `DECISION <date> <who>: <what> — because <why>` line per choice
made in dialogue, written before the next step. The conversation is what a
compaction summarizes; the plan is what `process_context.py` prints back at
every re-hydration — in full only while it is the current work, so the plan
carries `issue: <ref>` and the branch is named after the issue
(`process_context.py --issue N` otherwise).

Next: `/execute`.
